\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The graph half-turn fixes the quadratic center}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{The [alternating-cycle half-turn](fdip-000U)
#{P=(02)(13)} acts on the rational configuration space,
preserving the counting pairing, the action and the opposite-vertex
splitting. Its determinant is #{+1}. For every rational #{t>0},
its induced Clifford automorphism fixes the even center
pointwise. This action is different from the nontrivial
involution of that quadratic algebra.}

\p{More generally, let #{q} be a nondegenerate rational quadratic
form of positive even rank #{n}, and let #{T} be a #{q}-isometry.
Choose a rational orthogonal basis #{e_1,\ldots,e_n} and put
#{w=\iota_q(e_1)\cdots\iota_q(e_n)}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
Z(\operatorname{Cl}^+(q))&=\mathbb Q\cdot1\oplus\mathbb Q\cdot w,\\
\operatorname{Cl}(T)(w)&=(\det T)w.
\end{aligned}}
Thus determinant #{+1} fixes the center pointwise, while determinant
#{-1} induces its nontrivial involution.}

\p{For the center description, use the orthogonal Clifford monomial
basis. A basis monomial in the even algebra commutes with
#{\iota_q(e_i)\iota_q(e_j)}, for #{i\ne j}, precisely when its
index set contains both indices or neither. Every generator square
is nonzero, so these pairs are invertible and their conjugations
act diagonally on that basis. A central linear combination can
therefore involve only the empty and full index sets, since their
membership indicators must agree for every pair. Conversely both
#{1} and #{w} commute with every even word. They are independent
because #{n>0}. This gives the stated center and is the general
even-rank center description in TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.mem_center_even_iff_exists_eq_add_smul_volume},
under its nonempty even nonisotropic orthogonal spanning-list hypotheses over a
field of characteristic different from two.}

\p{For the action, consider the alternating multilinear map
##{A(v_1,\ldots,v_n)=\frac1{n!}
 \sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)
 \iota_q(v_{\sigma(1)})\cdots\iota_q(v_{\sigma(n)}).}
On an orthogonal list anticommutation makes this its ordered
Clifford product. Both #{(e_i)} and #{(Te_i)} are orthogonal,
so alternation gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cl}(T)(w)&=A(Te_1,\ldots,Te_n)\\
 &=(\det T)A(e_1,\ldots,e_n)=(\det T)w.
\end{aligned}}
Nondegeneracy gives #{(\det T)^2=1}. The center is quadratic
étale since #{w^2\ne0}; sending #{w} to #{-w} is its unique
nontrivial involution. This determinant-action argument is separate
from the cited formal center description.}

\p{To check the assertion in the actual family, let
#{h_1,\ldots,h_4} be the columns of the matrix #{H} in
\ref{fdip-000U}. They are a rational orthogonal basis for
#{q_t}; their nonzero squares are
#{4,12,4(2t+1),4(2t+3)}. Direct application of the half-turn gives
##{\begin{aligned}
Ph_1&=h_1,& Ph_2&=-h_2,\\
Ph_3&=-h_3,& Ph_4&=h_4.
\end{aligned}}
The four-dimensional [center calculation](fcap-0043)
identifies the center with the span of #{1} and
##{w=\iota_{q_t}(h_1)\iota_{q_t}(h_2)
      \iota_{q_t}(h_3)\iota_{q_t}(h_4).}
Its image under #{P} is #{(+1)(-1)(-1)(+1)w=w}.
Scalars are fixed as well, proving the assertion.}

\p{On the same center, the canonical nontrivial algebra
involution sends #{w\mapsto-w}. Its square is the identity
and it is nontrivial since #{1,w} are linearly independent.
For a field center it is quadratic Galois conjugation; for
#{\mathbb Q\times\mathbb Q} it exchanges the factors.
Thus the two-element graph group and the two-element group
of center automorphisms have different specified actions:
the former acts trivially on the center, whereas the latter
acts nontrivially. The center character in
\ref{fdip-0049} records the arithmetic Galois action, not
this supplied graph symmetry.}
